{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["And the verse's saying <b>then let him count, etc.</b> — Behold, since the sale is on account of our iniquities, as it says (Isaiah 50:1) ״הֵן בַּעֲוֹנוֹתֵיכֶם נִמְכַּרְתֶּם״, and the iniquity is a debt upon the person, at the time of tearing the deed one must make a reckoning of the debt that is upon him and pay the remainder. And that is the verse's saying <b>then let him reckon the years of his sale</b> — the years that remain while the house is still sold, and he shall deduct according to the proportion of the years from the iniquities of Israel, and the surplus he shall return — in the manner they said, may their memory be for blessing (Yoma 86b), concerning the laws of repentance and its rectification: the repentance of the weight — if a person derived benefit from the iniquity, he should afflict himself to the degree of the benefit he derived from the sin, and thereby he pays off his debt. And likewise the Master of the Universe will do when the time of redemption draws near, and this is their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Sanhedrin 98b), that there will be birth pangs of the Messiah. And by this <b>and he shall return to his possession</b> — meaning, he returns to the Holy One, blessed be He, and likewise Israel does, in the manner of the verse (Deuteronomy 30:3) ״וְשָׁב ה׳ אֶת שְׁבוּתְךָ״, and they said, may their memory be for blessing (Megillah 29b): יָשִׁיב was not said, but rather ״וְשָׁב״, etc."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:21.557139233Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}